   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">As of June 2023, the multi-donor trust fund total value is USD264.3 million, of which Australia has provided USD25.9 million.
&#x200d;
The GWSP-supported Climate and Economic Analyses for Resilience in Water (CLEAR) is a standardised methodological framework that supports rapid diagnostic analysis of country-level water and climate-related challenges and opportunities and has played an important role in making the case for the prioritisation of water in the country climate development reports (CCDRs). CLEAR links users to global and country-specific data on hydrology, climatology, demographics, institutional characteristics, public expenditure, water quality, water and sanitation access, and the role of water in the economy, and also considers each government&#x2019;s Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs). Its objective is to guide policy makers in quantifying the degree to which water sector challenges impose a binding constraint on development, and to provide the basis for identifying priority investment and policy actions.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The GWSP is a multi-donor water partnership with the World Bank which leads global research on water and supports the World Bank&#x2019;s $30 billion portfolio of water projects. The Trust Fund is valued at USD264.3 million (as of June 2023), of which Australia has provided USD25.9 million. 
&#x200d;
GWSP supports critical country-level analysis to help inform the World Bank&apos;s country climate development reports (CCDRs) and shape policy recommendations. Water challenges are one of the five most-mentioned challenges in CCDRs. The growing frequency and intensity of droughts and floods (short-term impacts) and increasing water scarcity (a long-term stressor) are discussed in all 36 CCDRs completed to end of June 2023.  For example, CCDR analysis indicates that investing in drought-related climate adaptation infrastructure could reduce GDP losses by almost 60 percent in Morocco.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
